// BT-3806 (ADR 0131 §6): the REPL keeps no escape check. A block that writes
// a session binding and is the value of a REPL expression goes to the display,
// not to a caller, and the expression list is not a method, so §6's
// "returned / stored / placed in a literal" rule (a compile error in class,
// value-type and actor methods since BT-3756/BT-3806) is skipped here.
//
// What it does today (probed): every such block is accepted and displayed,
// and the first later `value` raises the same "captures mutable state" error
// a method's escaping block raised before §6; the binding is never written.
// Whether the REPL should reject these at compile time instead is an open
// question for the maintainer (CLAUDE.md: REPL behaviour changes need
// confirmation), tracked as a follow-up to BT-3806. This case pins today's
// behaviour so that decision changes it on purpose.

t := 0
// => 0

// The expression's value is the block.
[t := t + 1]
// => Block/1

b := [t := t + 1]
// => Block/1

b value
// => ERROR: Cannot call 'value' on Block via perform:

t
// => 0

// The value of a conditional arm.
c := true ifTrue: [[t := t + 10]] ifFalse: [[0]]
// => Block/1

c value
// => ERROR: Cannot call 'value' on Block via perform:

t
// => 0

// An element of a collection literal.
d := #([t := t + 100])
// => ["Block/1"]

d first value
// => ERROR: Cannot call 'value' on Block via perform:

t
// => 0
